---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import AgentRun from '../../components/AgentRun.astro';
import TechTag from '../../components/TechTag.astro';
const stack = ['Python', 'DSPy', 'OpenAI', 'Flask', 'Langfuse', 'Google Sheets API', 'LINE Messaging API', 'Discord', 'PyTest', 'uv'];
---
<Base wide title="LINE Order Extraction Agent — Chi-Yi Hsu"
      description="An LLM agent that turns a messy LINE group chat into clean, verified gift-box orders.">
  <p class="muted"><a href="/projects/">Projects</a> · Side project · 2025–2026</p>
  <h1>LINE Order Extraction Agent</h1>
  <p>
    A small fruit gift-box shop took orders in a LINE group chat and retyped every one into a spreadsheet.
    Customers split one order over five messages, change quantities halfway, send gifts to two addresses,
    and chat without buying. This agent reads the raw conversation, extracts every order that was actually
    placed, and writes clean rows to Google Sheets, grouped by ship date.
  </p>

  <h2>Watch it run</h2>
  <p class="muted">A replay of a recorded pipeline run. Customer names, phone numbers and addresses are fictional
     Berlin examples and partly masked; prices are shown in euros. The stages and what each one decided are from the real run.</p>
  <AgentRun />


  <h2>How it works</h2>
  <ol>
    <li><strong>Clean the input</strong> — strip emoji and noise before the model sees the text.</li>
    <li><strong>Extract</strong> — one LLM call over the whole conversation; the DSPy signature is the spec:
        the last amendment wins, cancelled orders are dropped.</li>
    <li><strong>Second pass for missed orders</strong> — the model re-reads the log against what pass one found.
        It usually returns an empty list, and that empty list is what the extra call pays for.</li>
    <li><strong>Normalise</strong> — map items to options that actually exist, build one identity key per order
        (phone and date normalised) so duplicates are caught.</li>
    <li><strong>Write safely</strong> — verify the sheet's columns before every write, so a schema change fails
        loudly instead of putting phone numbers into the address column.</li>
    <li><strong>Trace</strong> — one Langfuse session per order, to see exactly which call produced which value.</li>
  </ol>

  <h2>Design decisions</h2>
  <ul>
    <li>Pay for a second LLM pass on every import: a missed order costs the shop a customer, an extra call costs cents.</li>
    <li>The model only returns a spec and a count; prices and product names come from code, never from the model.</li>
    <li>Date tabs are a projection of the main sheet, not a copy, so there is one source of truth.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Status — what is not built yet</h2>
  <p class="muted">
    The LINE webhook is wired but not deployed, so chat logs are pasted in by hand today; the daily push of the
    packing summary and an auto-learning loop are designed but not finished. The demo lists these openly.
  </p>

  <div class="tags">{stack.map((s) => <TechTag name={s} size="chip" />)}</div>
  <p style="margin-top:24px"><a href="https://github.com/steven91007/lineorderllm">Source on GitHub</a></p>
</Base>
